UNIT canonical_print
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@20bdd3dd041a cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs — print(parse(text).ast), the parse in its default (legacy) mode; the text every PRIMARY-SHA256 binding hashes
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG canonical_print(text: text) -> text
ENVELOPE any text (Unicode scalar values; no lone surrogates). The function is total: no text throws, and every malformed
  document degrades silently by the drops of TABLE LINE-CLASS. Non-text input is OUT.
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Re-print a SIR document in its one canonical layout: each directive on its own line at column 0 as its name,
  its arguments, then its attributes; each continuation on its own line after exactly two spaces; comments, blank lines and
  lines that belong to no directive dropped; lines joined by LF, and a final LF always.
BEHAVIOR
R1 Characters. WS is the charset WS and BREAK the charset BREAK (every BREAK character is also WS). To trim a text is to
  remove all its leading and trailing WS characters. Indentation is SPACE and TAB only. Everything counts Unicode scalar
  values; no rule depends on an encoding.
R2 Lines. The text is split at every LF (no LF: one line; a final LF leaves an empty last line). Each line loses ONE trailing
  CR when it has one; any other CR stays in the line as an ordinary character (it is WS and BREAK). Lines are classified in
  order by TABLE LINE-CLASS. The current directive is none until the first directive line.
R3 Each line splits into INDENT, its maximal leading run of SPACE and TAB (possibly empty), and CONTENT, the rest.
R4 Trailing comment. CODE is CONTENT cut at the FIRST position p where all hold: the character at p is WS; the character
  after it is the NUMBER SIGN; the character after that is WS or there is none; no BREAK character lies before p; no BREAK
  character lies after the NUMBER SIGN. At such a p, CODE = the characters before p with trailing WS removed, and the mark
  and all after it are dropped. Without such a p, CODE = CONTENT unchanged, trailing WS kept. A NUMBER SIGN glued to the
  text before it or after it is ordinary text. CITED FIDELITY package — legacy mode drops the comment silently
R5 Directive line. Of a column-0 CODE: HEAD = its maximal leading run of non-WS characters (empty when CODE is empty or
  starts with WS); REST = what follows HEAD once the WS run after HEAD is skipped. It is a directive line when HEAD is not
  empty, HEAD matches the grammar NAME, and REST holds no BREAK character. The directive's name is HEAD without its colon
  (when it has one); ATTRIBUTES (R8) of REST gives its args and attributes; it is appended and becomes the current
  directive. The name need not be a known directive: every name prints alike CITED FIDELITY package
R6 LOOSE line (column 0, not a directive line): with a current directive, CODE is appended to its continuations VERBATIM —
  not trimmed, no attributes taken out, any BREAK kept; with none, the line is dropped. So a byte order mark, a no-break space
  or any other non-SPACE non-TAB WS at column 0 makes the line LOOSE (a document whose first line starts with a byte order mark
  loses that line). CITED FIDELITY package
R7 Continuation (INDENT not empty, a current directive exists): ATTRIBUTES (R8) of CODE gives a body and attributes. The
  body is appended to the current directive's continuations (an empty body prints as two spaces alone). The attributes are
  MERGED into the directive's: a key it already has takes the new value in place (its print position stays, the old value
  is gone); a new key goes after the existing ones. CITED FIDELITY package
R8 ATTRIBUTES(s) gives a body and an ordered list of attributes. The keywords are the three upper-case words "CITED",
  "EARNED" and "ANCHOR". Start with body = s and no attributes, then repeat: find the LEFTMOST position i in body where the
  character at i is WS, the maximal WS run starting at i is followed directly by a keyword K, and the text A after K splits
  as W then T, W one or more WS characters and T one or more characters holding no BREAK. If there is none, stop. Else the
  value is A trimmed (it may be empty); when K is already in this call's list its value becomes the old value, one space,
  the new value, else K is added at the end; body is cut to the characters before i; repeat. Finally body is trimmed.
  So the first qualifying keyword swallows the rest of the line, other keywords included; a keyword at the very start of s,
  glued to a non-WS character, or followed by fewer than two characters, is body text. CITED FIDELITY package
R9 A BREAK can block a keyword on one pass and free it on a later one (the cut removed the BREAK), so a later keyword can be
  listed before an earlier one, and a key can collect two values. CITED FIDELITY package
R10 Print. Each directive, in order, prints one line: its name; then, when args is not empty, one space and args; then for
  each attribute in order one space, the keyword, one space and the value (an empty value leaves a trailing space). Each of
  its continuations follows in order on its own line: two spaces, then the continuation. The lines are joined with LF and
  one final LF is added; with no directive the result is a single LF.
TABLE LINE-CLASS — first matching row; CONTENT, CODE and INDENT as R3–R4
  the line is empty after trimming                          | blank: skipped
  CONTENT starts with the NUMBER SIGN                       | full-line comment: skipped; the current directive is unchanged
  INDENT empty and a directive line (R5)                    | a new directive (R5)
  INDENT empty, otherwise                                   | LOOSE (R6)
  INDENT not empty and no current directive                 | dropped
  INDENT not empty                                          | continuation (R7)
PINNED charset WS = hex code points 0009-000D 0020 00A0 1680 2000-200A 2028 2029 202F 205F 3000 FEFF CITED FIDELITY package — the host's whitespace class; 0085 (NEXT LINE) is NOT in it
PINNED charset BREAK = hex code points 000A 000D 2028 2029 CITED FIDELITY package — LF, CR, LINE SEPARATOR, PARAGRAPH SEPARATOR
PINNED grammar NAME = one upper-case ASCII letter, then zero or more upper-case ASCII letters, ASCII digits or "-", then at most one ":" CITED FIDELITY package — "UNIT:" names UNIT, "Ab:" and "A::" do not match
INVARIANT the result ends with LF; cut at every LF, each piece before the final LF starts with an upper-case ASCII letter
  (a directive) or with two spaces (a continuation), and the first starts with a letter. The result is a single LF exactly
  when no line is a directive line.
OUT-OF-DOMAIN no text throws. Malformed SIR degrades silently: lines before the first directive are dropped, a CR-only
  document is one line that is never a directive line (REST holds a BREAK) and prints as a single LF, an interior BREAK in a
  column-0 line makes it LOOSE, and the parse's errors and warnings never reach the result.
ORACLE value -> oracles/canonical_print.json
SCOPE IN: R1–R10 over any text. OUT: strict mode, the parse's errors, warnings, indents and comment bodies, the directive
  table's register and kind fields, the hash itself, and lone surrogates.
PROVENANCE rederive@20bdd3dd041a (MIT), cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs: parse (legacy mode), attrsOf, splitTrailingComment,
  isDirectiveName, print; seam selfhost/seams/canonical_print.mjs. Cold cut 2026-10-26 into the v0.5 contract register from
  the pinned authority snapshot.
